WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.680
Hey there, welcome to the Paws at Data Science Hangout. I'm Libby Herron, and this is a recording

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.040
of our weekly community call that happens every Thursday at 12 p.m. U.S. Eastern Time.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.280
If you are not joining us live, you miss out on the amazing chat that's going on. So find

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.719
the link in the description where you can add our call to your calendar and come hang

00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:36.360
out with the most supportive, friendly, and funny data community you'll ever experience.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.360
Can't wait to see you there.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.880
In celebration of five years of the Hangout, I am so excited to introduce you to our featured

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.439
leader today. We are joined by Hubert Hickman, Senior Data Strategist and Data Operations

00:00:48.439 --> 00:00:54.160
Lead at the University of Chicago's Data for the Common Good, which is just such a cool

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.959
phrase, Data for the Common Good. I love it so much. Hubert, welcome, but not really

00:00:58.959 --> 00:01:02.520
welcome because you've been joining the Hangout for so long, and a lot of people know your

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.519
face and your name. I would love it if you could introduce yourself, tell us a little

00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:13.199
bit about what you do, and something that you like to do for fun.

00:01:13.199 --> 00:01:19.919
Thank you, Libby, and all the Paws at folks for this Hangout. It's a great place. I am

00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:25.480
Hubert Hickman. As Libby said, I'm the Senior Data Strategist and Data Operations Lead for

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:33.239
Data for the Common Good, also known as D4CG at the University of Chicago. We are the home

00:01:33.239 --> 00:01:39.639
of the Pediatric Cancer Data Commons, among other initiatives, but the PCDC, Pediatric

00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:46.519
Cancer Data Commons, is a consortia of consortia, 16 different pediatric cancer types, all rolled

00:01:46.519 --> 00:01:55.199
into an umbrella, but each with their own data and governance structures, but unified

00:01:55.199 --> 00:02:02.760
into the PCDC. The data is not ours. We hold the data. It is governed by the consortia

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:09.679
themselves. Our data dictionaries are open. You can go and look at them. And so there's

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:14.279
a lot of work that goes in within our group to putting those data dictionaries together,

00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:19.839
getting the data in, wizardry on governance and agreements and data use agreements and that sort

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:26.320
of stuff. There's also other initiatives like penetrating brain injuries, rare epilepsies,

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.240
and other things that are happening. I, by training, I am a very mathy computer scientist.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:40.119
I am one of those folks who did not complete a PhD. I think there's a number of us here on

00:02:40.119 --> 00:02:48.240
the Hangout that are in that state. My early career was industrial wastewater treatment,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:54.279
college administration, defense and power engineering. And since 1993, I've been working

00:02:54.279 --> 00:03:01.880
in clinical and research and dramatics. I got into this area because of a friendship with a

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:07.839
liver transplant surgeon here in Omaha. Eventually, one thing led to another. Because of that

00:03:07.839 --> 00:03:15.600
friendship, I co-founded a company with addition of a grad school buddy in my basement dealing

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:22.759
with caring for and transplant patients and helping centers deal with all the logistics

00:03:22.759 --> 00:03:27.759
and clinical things that were needed. That company started in my basement, stayed there

00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:38.440
for two years. 17 years later, we had 65 transplant sites across the US, Canada, and Australia. And I

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:45.399
think about a third of the transplant patients were in our systems in the US. After the company

00:03:45.399 --> 00:03:52.600
sold, in the meantime, in 2003, I worked as an employee at UNMC working on the NICU system,

00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:05.520
the new things that led to a successful system. In 2012, the EPIC came in, our team was no longer

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:12.360
needed for some of the charting for the physician documentation system at UNMC. And so I sort of

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:17.000
rattled around a little bit after the company sold with that, got into the research informatics side

00:04:17.119 --> 00:04:23.320
from a call from another doctor, a different doctor at UNMC that wanted help setting up a

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:30.160
coordinate node at UNMC, Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Network, one of the national research

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:39.920
networks. And so that got me into the research side, ITV2, the research data warehouse. That

00:04:40.399 --> 00:04:49.799
led in 2015 to another company finding me that did the same sort of work at NYU Langone, ITV2

00:04:49.799 --> 00:04:57.399
primarily in research data. That led, another step led to some initial work with the National

00:04:57.399 --> 00:05:04.920
Cancer Institute in 2017. That project was called DI3, integrating imagery, image data,

00:05:04.959 --> 00:05:11.119
multimodal image data, along with research study data. That's the first time I'd actually

00:05:11.119 --> 00:05:17.519
heard of the Chicago group as I knew it as then because they were also one of the other groups

00:05:17.519 --> 00:05:24.600
working on this project. That project took ITV2, research study data, integrated that,

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.760
that's also where I ran into R because I needed a thing that could write SAS export files for

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:36.239
SDTM, study data tabulation model files, and I need a little web server to write extensions for

00:05:36.239 --> 00:05:41.640
what, for ITV2. And I found this thing called R and Shiny and then, hmm, this will fill the bill,

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:47.559
and it did. That led to more work with the National Cancer Institute within the clinical

00:05:47.559 --> 00:05:54.079
trial reporting program, dealing with structuring of unstructured clinical trial inclusion and

00:05:54.079 --> 00:06:03.200
exclusion criteria and trial search space. That was my primary focus for the time at NCI. And in

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:11.920
early 2024, I moved to the D4CG group. It is a, you know, it is a wonderful group of folks. We're

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:21.480
doing really great work in areas where that work is really needed. I think I've written code in

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:27.160
16 different programming languages over the years. I counted them up, and I keep thinking good ones

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:35.040
every now and then. I will, yeah, I will say this. I have been very fortunate in my career that I,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:42.519
you know, I have made a difference and done good work. And, you know, there's a combination of

00:06:42.519 --> 00:06:49.880
factors that led to that, but. Well, there's a couple of things I heard in there that I would

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.480
love to, like, pick back up and just ask you some more questions about. One is you said you didn't

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:02.799
complete a PhD, which I think that a lot of people can relate to, and that you came from a more

00:07:02.799 --> 00:07:10.920
software engineer mathy background. So you've come to data science from, like, the side, right? Not

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:16.359
from, straight up from statistics. And the other one is that you did not have a background in

00:07:16.359 --> 00:07:25.119
cancer research. You got here because you made a friend, right? You sort of found somebody who

00:07:25.119 --> 00:07:30.239
pulled you in to help you solve a problem. And you said yes to, sure, I'll help you solve this

00:07:30.239 --> 00:07:37.640
problem that I know nothing about. So could you tell me a little bit about how you met that friend?

00:07:37.799 --> 00:07:41.799
Did you join a community like this one? What did that look like?

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:52.200
We, in 1990, bought a computer called the Next Workstation. If you remember what Steve Jobs did

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:58.760
between getting fired from Apple and getting rehired to Apple, he did Next. And it was a

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:04.279
wonderful system, especially compared to the state-of-the-art of today. It had lots of power,

00:08:04.279 --> 00:08:11.959
a wonderful GUI, networking. It was Unix underneath. So it was, for me, it was like,

00:08:11.959 --> 00:08:18.440
oh, that's my dream computer. And this transplant surgeon, unbeknownst to me, had bought one of

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:24.200
these machines too. And I had set up a little user group, and I think there were like five of us in

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:31.079
Omaha. It was not a big community. And that's how I met him. And they had had IBM Enterprise

00:08:31.079 --> 00:08:38.440
Alliance come in to make a smoke and mirrors demo system for them. So they had had two different

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:45.799
unsuccessful runs at building a system to meet their needs. And when we said, yes, oh, yeah,

00:08:45.799 --> 00:08:52.359
we can do this, we were very naive. We said, oh, look. You didn't have a company at that point,

00:08:52.359 --> 00:08:58.679
right? You were just like, I think we can tackle this problem to help you manage these transplant

00:08:58.679 --> 00:09:05.559
patients. Right. And we said, oh, three months. It took us two years to build that system out.

00:09:05.559 --> 00:09:12.919
So there was a lot of learning and deep learning. There was no clinical background in my history,

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:18.039
but you have to learn and listen and watch how people work, what the needs were,

00:09:18.679 --> 00:09:24.200
and juggle all the other things to make a go of it. And so, yeah, it was a challenge.

00:09:24.919 --> 00:09:28.359
But fortunately, my friend was a very patient man. So.

00:09:29.159 --> 00:09:36.119
So you've been building data solutions for many, many years. I'm wondering what advice you would

00:09:36.119 --> 00:09:42.039
give somebody who is coming in new to the game and is being tasked with building a data solution

00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:46.840
for somebody? Because my advice is always go sit with them, shadow them, watch them work for a

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:52.760
really long time and ask them a bunch of questions. How long did you spend just watching the way that

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:57.799
people were interacting with their existing solution for those patients?

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:04.520
Oh, a very long time. I mean, we did interviews upon interviews, watched them work. I made cassette

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:10.039
tapes back in the day of all these so I could replay them because I didn't know. It's like

00:10:10.039 --> 00:10:16.119
I give the analogy of Homer's dog and the Simpsons spot, you know, spot doesn't understand any word

00:10:16.119 --> 00:10:20.599
other than his name. You know, so it's like blah, blah, blah, spot, blah, blah, blah, spot.

00:10:20.599 --> 00:10:26.599
So that's the way I felt at first. So you have to be able, I think, to be successful in some

00:10:26.599 --> 00:10:32.280
of these arenas to where there's sort of this deep domain knowledge needed. You have to be able to

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:38.599
learn that domain well enough and step back and make a solution, you know, see what the real

00:10:38.599 --> 00:10:43.880
problems are. Because that's it's not necessarily the problems they're telling you what's in front

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.359
of them, but what are the patterns? Where are they inefficient? What do they need to see? And

00:10:48.359 --> 00:10:53.640
that's whether it's clinical or research, you know, some of those same questions apply. You've

00:10:53.640 --> 00:11:00.760
got to learn and be willing to learn enough of the domain to know what a system might offer

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:07.400
in terms of solving real problems for people, be it research or clinical workflows.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.719
Yeah, I wanted to call out something that Abigail just said in the chat,

00:11:11.719 --> 00:11:15.719
and then I'm going to read Abigail's question in the Slido. By the way, you can ask questions in

00:11:15.719 --> 00:11:21.239
the Slido, everybody, don't forget. Because Abigail said these days that she does a lot more

00:11:21.239 --> 00:11:26.039
just build the thing and put it in front of somebody now because the building part has gotten

00:11:26.039 --> 00:11:33.719
so much faster and then people can respond to it. I'm wondering if you have seen AI-driven

00:11:33.719 --> 00:11:38.280
development sort of shift that building bespoke stuff game a little bit.

00:11:39.239 --> 00:11:47.000
It has. And that's, you know, AI is an accelerant for good or for evil or for bad things. You know,

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:52.039
you can use it in a good way and a bad way. And the bad way, the good way, excuse me,

00:11:52.039 --> 00:11:59.799
the good way is just what Abigail mentioned, that it makes putting a prototype together

00:11:59.799 --> 00:12:06.200
really easy. One of the things I'm working on now in D4CG is, you know, we're working on

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:12.679
direct from patient data via FHIR and research data. And, you know, some of that data is ugly.

00:12:13.479 --> 00:12:20.440
And, you know, the EHR data is all over the place and things are here, things are there.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:27.479
It's not a straightforward or easy issue to solve. But once you get the data part of this figured out,

00:12:27.479 --> 00:12:32.679
you know, what the data should look like, what's the shape, what's the structure of the data,

00:12:32.679 --> 00:12:38.359
and I still think you need to have a model of that in your head, at least, of what this data,

00:12:38.359 --> 00:12:44.440
what the data are all about. You know, you can literally, I mean, what I did for one of my,

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:50.679
one of my current projects is the first version of this. I just drew pictures for Claude and said,

00:12:50.679 --> 00:12:57.080
here's the data, here's what I want you to do. And it did a really bad first version of that.

00:12:57.159 --> 00:13:02.919
Yeah, it wasn't very good at all. But it put it in front of my face to see it. And then you can

00:13:02.919 --> 00:13:09.719
realize, oh, this is not so good. But then you can go and start cycling and iterating that to

00:13:09.719 --> 00:13:14.359
make it better. And then you put it in front of people now, oh, wow, this is nice. And so,

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:18.919
that thing that would have taken me months and months and months and months and months to write,

00:13:18.919 --> 00:13:25.880
if I had to do it myself, Claude's perfectly happy doing crazy JavaScript stuff and wiring

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:32.039
in shiny reactives and whatnot to make things work. And it's perfectly, you know, it does a

00:13:32.039 --> 00:13:38.200
good job. Now, you still have to ultimately go and write a lot of LLM code and sort of de-slop it

00:13:38.919 --> 00:13:45.239
once you're all done with it. De-slopping, how unfortunate. It's true. Okay, well, Abigail's

00:13:45.239 --> 00:13:50.039
question was about programming languages, because you mentioned that you work in 16 programming

00:13:50.039 --> 00:13:55.080
languages. And I just asked you about AI, which means that you have seen a lot of evolution over

00:13:55.080 --> 00:14:00.840
the years and a lot of change in technology, and you've had to navigate all of that. Hats off to

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:05.880
you, because it's hard for me, and I only know a handful of programming languages, and not very

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:11.080
well. So, Abigail's question was, what was your favorite programming language and your least

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:16.520
favorite programming languages and why? And are there any that you wish you could still work in

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:22.039
that maybe you don't get to work in anymore? Oh, that's a good question. Least favorite?

00:14:22.599 --> 00:14:28.200
I would... I'm going to say I do not like working in Java. I've worked in it, but it's just not my

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:33.880
favorite. It seems wordy, and I know lots of people love it, and it's efficient. They work in it day

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:40.599
on, day out, but it's just not my cup of tea. I'm not a big Java fan. Favorites depends upon the

00:14:40.599 --> 00:14:46.039
time you ask. I've had many favorites over the years, but mainly those which allow me to get

00:14:46.039 --> 00:14:55.000
my work done without getting in the way, and I like Python. Both Python and R for that. I do

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:02.679
most of the GUI work I do in R. It just makes... I know how Shiny works. The way reactives and

00:15:02.679 --> 00:15:10.119
interactivity work is straightforward enough that you can get what you need done without requiring

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:20.359
lots and lots and lots of work. In Python, I first ran across that in 2001 or so, back in 1.5 Python

00:15:20.359 --> 00:15:27.559
days, and I could just almost think Python when you started writing Python. And when we did the

00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:33.479
system in 2003 at UNMC, that was written in Python, and that was not a popular choice at the time. I

00:15:33.479 --> 00:15:39.080
had lots of people wander by my office, wander by my office, and they'd say, oh, I'm going to

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:47.479
be writing this in .NET or Java or whatever. But it's like, nope, we're going to do it this way.

00:15:47.479 --> 00:15:55.080
Now Python runs most of the world, but yeah, those are my answers. And other languages I've

00:15:55.080 --> 00:16:02.280
certainly enjoyed. You don't stay in it. Some of those languages are dead now. Some are still

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:10.760
around. But I think Python and R are my favorites. Java.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:11.640
Some will never die.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:16.119
Some will never die. Perl, I can write fine, but you can't read it later.

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:21.320
Someone said in the chat, Pauline said, I enjoy writing in Perl, and I miss that.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:24.760
The one I was thinking about was COBOL, that just it never dies, because there's so much

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:31.960
that's written in COBOL that needs to survive for business purposes. I feel like every

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:36.760
business that's been around for at least 50 years needs to have at least one COBOL person.

00:16:38.039 --> 00:16:41.719
At least that's in my experience. Well, Arsenis, I saw you piggybacked

00:16:41.719 --> 00:16:45.239
on to Abigail's question. Would you like to ask that one live?

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.520
Before I close Abigail's question. All right. Awesome.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:59.479
Sure. Thanks. So about programming languages still, is there one or a couple, and I think

00:16:59.479 --> 00:17:04.760
you just mentioned R and Python, but are they the ones that you prefer for data science

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:10.839
more specifically versus other software engineering tasks and things of that nature?

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:18.040
Yeah, I think for the data science-y part, I think R. You can do it in R and Python.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:25.880
A lot of people doing Python. My own preferences are just because it's a little more concise

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:40.680
and data frames kind of work consistently within the ecosystem. But it's the conciseness of it all,

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:47.959
and plus just the wide range of things that are available. Whatever you need, there's a library

00:17:47.959 --> 00:17:53.319
for that probably in data science. And I think Python may have caught up a lot of that way,

00:17:54.040 --> 00:18:00.040
but my personal use of Python is sort of the backend use case, data processing,

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:08.599
that versus, okay, let's do presentation. I did one of the apps that I've written for PCDC.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:19.479
It is for the PCDC, but DC4G is a pivot table app, which uses the Chinese JavaScript pivot table,

00:18:19.479 --> 00:18:27.000
the drag and drop pivot table widget. Then it's an internal app, but it's a good example. Okay,

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:31.959
I use Python to manipulate the data coming out to get it into the structure I needed. But

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:37.000
yeah, pivot table, all this other stuff, you can see heat maps, graphs, this, that,

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:45.000
all the other things. That was not much code to get a lot of functionality out of things,

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:53.079
but I would say R. And I think in terms of what people are learning these days, folks coming out

00:18:53.079 --> 00:18:59.959
of college, I think R is more and more popular for the data science community as well. Because

00:18:59.959 --> 00:19:05.880
it's open, you can go get it, like SAS or some of the other things that cost money, or you get

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:12.839
some version that's not really for commercial use or something. I love it. Very good answer.

00:19:13.319 --> 00:19:17.560
Thank you so much for asking that piggyback question. And thank you, Abigail, for asking

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:23.560
your original question as well. And we have so many questions popping up in the Slido now. Thank

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:31.160
you, everybody. I would love it if we could ask Natasha's question really quickly, which is one

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:37.079
of my favorites. She always asks, what does an average day look like for you? Or maybe an average

00:19:37.079 --> 00:19:45.800
week, if every day is not the same? Yeah, I think a lot of what I do on a day-to-day basis,

00:19:46.839 --> 00:19:54.680
within an average week, I rotate. I work with the data standards group, developers group,

00:19:55.239 --> 00:19:59.800
I work in their research group. So there's many different groups that are very internal,

00:20:00.599 --> 00:20:10.119
that I sort of float between and in. And so there's what I would call standard sort of,

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:16.599
I need to get this code written to do these things. And I've got features that need to go

00:20:16.599 --> 00:20:21.479
into an app I'm working on, or there's some backend thing, or I need to update one of the

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:29.880
things that's one of the apps that I support. So there's that part of the aspect. The other part

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:37.000
of it is being in what were the kinds of things we should be doing at a higher level in the group,

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:45.160
just participating in that, throwing out research ideas and everything, and thinking about what

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:52.119
things should come, how, what are out there in terms of opportunities for us to do things,

00:20:52.119 --> 00:20:57.400
throwing ideas out on the table. And not all of Hubert's ideas are great, but I'm going to throw

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:05.319
them out on the table to say, well, what about this idea or that idea, and pull things together.

00:21:05.319 --> 00:21:10.119
And to support other sort of group questions that may come my way, or someone asks, well,

00:21:10.119 --> 00:21:17.719
what do you think about this, that? So it's a mix of things. I'm not a always software developer,

00:21:17.719 --> 00:21:24.280
or data, always data person, or always other person. So it's a mix of different things

00:21:24.839 --> 00:21:29.160
that I do during the course of a week. And the days do, you know,

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:34.040
no days alike in my week because of meetings and other things.

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:42.199
I think that it's really hard for me to even, even say, average week, because throughout the

00:21:42.199 --> 00:21:46.119
year, things change so much, right? And if you work in a project based environment,

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:51.800
and a client based environment, things change constantly. I don't know if anybody else can

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:55.479
